  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery indicates the UP is now between a \ndownstream trough moving to New England and a mid level ridge over \nthe Rockies and Plains. At the sfc, weak high pressure is building \noverhead and will continue to hold influence over the Upper Great \nLakes into Thursday. Nighttime microphysics indicate areas of dense \nfog over western Lake Superior which have intruded over parts of the \nwestern spine of the UP thanks to light northerly flow. These \nvisibility impacts should dissipate shortly after sunrise as temps \nhold in the 50s. Meanwhile, a trough over the Pacific makes landfall \nlate today, moving over the Rockies and flattening the mid level \nridge Wednesday night into Thursday as it approaches the Great \nLakes. With this increase in low level WAA, widespread temps climb \ninto the low to mid 80s Wednesday and Thursday. The exceptions to \nthis are the lakeshores on Wednesday, which remain in the 70s \ncourtesy of lake breeze influences and a few interior western \nlocations expected to reach near 90 on Thursday prior to trough and \ncold fropa Thursday night into Friday. This also reintroduces PoPs \nto the UP Thursday which continue into Saturday. While sufficient \ninstability will be present for thunderstorms, bulk shear appears to \nbe lacking to yield severe weather potential at this time. Quieter \nand warm conditions return for early next week as ridging once again \nreturns to the region.",
